;; ===========================================================================
;; ROE_Deontic.kif
;;
;; The deontic layer of the ROE formalization. Load it after ROE_Master.kif,
;; ROE_Supplementary.kif and ROE_Series.kif, and only for experiments with the
;; deontic or modal reading of measures, never with the main test suite.
;;
;; The catalog relations state what a measure covers. They are not themselves
;; normative: a measure sitting in the compendium binds no one. The norm arises
;; when competent authority places the measure in force, and that is what the
;; SUMO relations &%permits and &%prohibits express here, asserted of the
;; issuing authority rather than of the measure.
;;
;; Law.kif makes permitting a formula and prohibiting it mutually exclusive.
;; Where a scenario puts in force an authorization and a prohibition naming the
;; same class, as rules 84 A and 84 D do, these two rules therefore make the
;; knowledge base inconsistent, and an inconsistent knowledge base proves every
;; query, including those a test expects to fail. The flag &%roeMeasureConflict
;; in ROE_Supplementary.kif identifies the cause but cannot prevent the
;; inconsistency. That is why this file is kept apart from the main knowledge
;; base. The authorization layer does not use these rules: &%permits and
;; &%prohibits take a reified &%Formula, where search cost concentrates, so
;; inference runs on &%roeActionAuthorized instead.
;;
;; The two rules are also weaker in meaning than an ROE measure, and are not a
;; basis for the results of the thesis. The permitted formula says only that
;; some act of class ?C may occur; it does not carry the conditions of the
;; measure, the particular act, or the operation. ?OP appears only in the
;; antecedent, so permissions granted for different operations become the same
;; deontic statement. A faithful deontic reading would need the operation, and
;; the conditions of the measure, inside the formula.
;; ===========================================================================

;; Rule 1: A measure in force confers a permission held by the authority.
(=>
  (and
    (roeInForce ?M ?OP)
    (roeMeasureAuthorizes ?M ?C)
    (roeIssuingAuthority ?OP ?AUTH))
  (permits ?AUTH
    (exists (?A)
      (instance ?A ?C))))

;; Rule 2: A measure in force imposes a prohibition held by the authority.
(=>
  (and
    (roeInForce ?M ?OP)
    (roeMeasureProhibits ?M ?C)
    (roeIssuingAuthority ?OP ?AUTH))
  (prohibits ?AUTH
    (exists (?A)
      (instance ?A ?C))))

;; ===========================================================================
;; Deontic rules from ROE_Master.kif
;;
;; Every rule of ROE_Master.kif that concluded &%permits, (not (permits ...))
;; or &%prohibits is collected here, under the heading of the item it came
;; from. Several pairs could not hold together: WeaponsFree Rule 1 permits an
;; attack on any Combatant, while the NoStrikeList, NonCombatant, HorsDeCombat
;; and FriendlyForce rules deny permission for the same formula, so a target
;; meeting both conditions made the main knowledge base inconsistent. The
;; self-defense permissions and Harassment Rule 3 formed another such pair;
;; they now state rights with &%holdsRight, and Harassment Rule 3 reaches only
;; a self-defense caused by the harassment, which a hostile act cannot be, so
;; the pair no longer conflicts. No test of the authorization layer used these
;; rules.
;;
;; HigherHeadquarters Rule 3 has been corrected on the way: it used an
;; &%Order, a &%Sentence, as the agent of a process; the act of ordering is an
;; &%Ordering.
;; ===========================================================================

;; --- from item 3. Harassment

;; Rule 3: Harassment does NOT give rise to a right of self-defense. The
;; captain of the harassed ship does not hold the right to a self-defense by
;; the crew against the harassing ship that is caused by the harassment. A
;; self-defense caused by something else, such as a hostile act at the same
;; time, is not reached. The right is the captain's: unit self-defense is the
;; right of the unit commander to defend the unit (Newport glossary).
(=>
  (and
    (instance ?H Harassment)
    (agent ?H ?A)
    (patient ?H ?T)
    (instance ?T Ship)
    (member ?A
      (ShipCrewFn ?S))
    (member ?CAPT
      (ShipCrewFn ?T))
    (attribute ?CAPT ShipCaptain))
  (not
    (holdsRight ?CAPT
      (exists (?SD ?X)
        (and
          (instance ?SD SelfDefense)
          (agent ?SD ?X)
          (member ?X
            (ShipCrewFn ?T))
          (patient ?SD ?S)
          (causes ?H ?SD))))))

;; --- Rights of self-defense
;;
;; Self-defense is an inherent right, so these rules use &%holdsRight, which
;; names the holder of a right and no source for it. &%permits is the act of
;; an agent with the power to prohibit (Law.kif), and &%confersRight names an
;; entity that grants the right; both fit authority granted by ROE, not an
;; inherent right. SUMO draws the same line in MilitaryProcesses.kif. The
;; holder must be a &%CognitiveAgent: for a ship it is the captain, and for a
;; State its chief of state or head of government. Until SUMO can represent
;; the intentions of an organization, the captain stands for the ship: the
;; ship acts as a cognitive agent through its captain, and through the captain
;; its crew.

;; --- from item 5. IndividualSelfDefense

;; Rule 2: A human who is the target of a hostile act or hostile intent holds
;; the right of individual self-defense against it
(=>
  (and
    (instance ?T HostileConduct)
    (patient ?T ?A)
    (instance ?A Human))
  (holdsRight ?A
    (exists (?S)
      (and
        (instance ?S IndividualSelfDefense)
        (agent ?S ?A)
        (causes ?T ?S)))))

;; --- from item 6. UnitSelfDefense

;; Rule 2: The captain of a ship holds the right to defend another ship of the
;; same military organization against an attack
(=>
  (and
    (instance ?T Attack)
    (patient ?T ?TARGET)
    (instance ?TARGET Ship)
    (instance ?SHIP Ship)
    (not
      (equal ?SHIP ?TARGET))
    (member ?CAPT
      (ShipCrewFn ?SHIP))
    (attribute ?CAPT ShipCaptain)
    (member ?CAPT2
      (ShipCrewFn ?TARGET))
    (attribute ?CAPT2 ShipCaptain)
    (instance ?ORG MilitaryOrganization)
    (member ?CAPT ?ORG)
    (member ?CAPT2 ?ORG))
  (holdsRight ?CAPT
    (exists (?S ?X)
      (and
        (instance ?S UnitSelfDefense)
        (agent ?S ?X)
        (member ?X
          (ShipCrewFn ?SHIP))
        (causes ?T ?S)))))

;; Rule 3: The captain of a ship that is the target of a hostile act or
;; hostile intent holds the right of unit self-defense against it
(=>
  (and
    (instance ?T HostileConduct)
    (patient ?T ?SHIP)
    (instance ?SHIP Ship)
    (member ?CAPT
      (ShipCrewFn ?SHIP))
    (attribute ?CAPT ShipCaptain))
  (holdsRight ?CAPT
    (exists (?S ?X)
      (and
        (instance ?S UnitSelfDefense)
        (agent ?S ?X)
        (member ?X
          (ShipCrewFn ?SHIP))
        (causes ?T ?S)))))

;; --- from item 7. DefenseOfOthers

;; Rule: When a higher headquarters designates persons who are not part of the
;; Force and the captain of a ship protects them, the headquarters confers on
;; the captain the right to defend them against a hostile act or hostile
;; intent. By Merge.kif, the captain then holds that right. Defense of others
;; is conferred, not inherent, because the persons to be defended are
;; specified by authority (Newport Series 12); for some States the right is
;; inherent under national law, which this rule does not represent. A friendly
;; unit of another State is not covered here: that is an extension of unit
;; self-defense under Series 11.
(=>
  (and
    (instance ?T HostileConduct)
    (patient ?T ?Y)
    (instance ?HQ HigherHeadquarters)
    (instance ?DES Designating)
    (agent ?DES ?HQ)
    (patient ?DES ?Y)
    (instance ?PR Protecting)
    (agent ?PR ?CAPT)
    (patient ?PR ?Y)
    (instance ?SHIP Ship)
    (member ?CAPT
      (ShipCrewFn ?SHIP))
    (attribute ?CAPT ShipCaptain))
  (confersRight ?HQ ?CAPT
    (exists (?S ?X)
      (and
        (instance ?S DefenseOfOthers)
        (agent ?S ?X)
        (member ?X
          (ShipCrewFn ?SHIP))
        (causes ?T ?S)))))

;; --- from item 8. NationalSelfDefense

;; Rule: The leader of a State that is the target of a hostile act or hostile
;; intent holds the right of national self-defense against it. This is the
;; inherent right recognized in Article 51 of the UN Charter (jus ad bellum).
;; Which office decides differs between States, hence the disjunction; the
;; Handbook says the decision is retained at the highest levels of
;; governmental or executive authority.
(=>
  (and
    (instance ?T HostileConduct)
    (patient ?T ?ST)
    (instance ?ST Nation)
    (or
      (chiefOfState ?ST ?POS ?LEADER)
      (headOfGovernment ?ST ?POS ?LEADER)))
  (holdsRight ?LEADER
    (exists (?S)
      (and
        (instance ?S NationalSelfDefense)
        (causes ?T ?S)))))

;; --- from item 9. CollectiveSelfDefense

;; Rule: If a State is the target of a hostile act or hostile intent, the
;; leader of an allied State holds the right of collective self-defense to
;; defend it (UN Charter, Article 51).
(=>
  (and
    (instance ?T HostileConduct)
    (patient ?T ?ALLY2)
    (instance ?ALLY2 Nation)
    (instance ?ALLY1 Nation)
    (not
      (equal ?ALLY1 ?ALLY2))
    (allied ?ALLY1 ?ALLY2)
    (or
      (chiefOfState ?ALLY1 ?POS ?LEADER)
      (headOfGovernment ?ALLY1 ?POS ?LEADER)))
  (holdsRight ?LEADER
    (exists (?CSD)
      (and
        (instance ?CSD CollectiveSelfDefense)
        (patient ?CSD ?ALLY2)
        (causes ?T ?CSD)))))

;; --- from item 10. Declared

;; Rule 2: A unit that has received the declaration is permitted by the
;; declaring authority to engage the declared hostile force
(=>
  (and
    (property ?F DeclaredHostileStatus)
    (instance ?F Physical)
    (instance ?DECL Directing)
    (instance ?HQ HigherHeadquarters)
    (agent ?DECL ?HQ)
    (patient ?DECL ?F)
    (destination ?DECL ?UNIT)
    (instance ?UNIT MilitaryOrganization))
  (permits ?HQ
    (exists (?E)
      (and
        (instance ?E Attack)
        (agent ?E ?UNIT)
        (patient ?E ?F)))))

;; --- from item 19b. VesselHailing

;; Rule 3: If the agent of a hailing doubts that the activity of the hailed
;; vessel is legal, the agent is obliged to report it to a higher
;; headquarters. Whether a boarding follows is the decision of that
;; headquarters, so the rule states an obligation to report, not a boarding.
(=>
  (and
    (instance ?H VesselHailing)
    (agent ?H ?A)
    (instance ?A CognitiveAgent)
    (destination ?H ?V)
    (instance ?ACT IntentionalProcess)
    (instrument ?ACT ?V)
    (overlapsTemporally
      (WhenFn ?ACT)
      (WhenFn ?H))
    (doubts ?A
      (modalAttribute
        (instrument ?ACT ?V)
        Legal)))
  (modalAttribute
    (exists (?REP ?HQ)
      (and
        (instance ?REP Stating)
        (agent ?REP ?A)
        (instance ?HQ HigherHeadquarters)
        (destination ?REP ?HQ)
        (refers ?REP ?ACT)
        (earlier
          (WhenFn ?H)
          (WhenFn ?REP))))
    Obligation))

;; --- requests for boarding (formerly under item 19. TacticalQuestioning)

;; Rule 3: If higher authority approves the requesting agent,
;; BoardingOperation is permitted
(=>
  (and
    (instance ?REQ Requesting)
    (agent ?REQ ?A)
    (instance ?CMD CognitiveAgent)
    (destination ?REQ ?CMD)
    (patient ?REQ ?BO)
    (instance ?BO BoardingOperation)
    (approves ?CMD ?A))
  (permits ?CMD
    (and
      (instance ?BO BoardingOperation)
      (agent ?BO ?A))))

;; --- from item 20. BoardingOperation

;; Rule 3: During a BoardingOperation, SearchOperation is permitted
(=>
  (and
    (instance ?BO BoardingOperation)
    (agent ?BO ?A)
    (instance ?A AutonomousAgent)
    (patient ?BO ?P)
    (instance ?P Physical))
  (permits ?A
    (exists (?S)
      (and
        (instance ?S SearchOperation)
        (eventLocated ?S ?P)
        (agent ?S ?A)
        (subProcess ?S ?BO)))))

;; --- from item 23. SearchOperation

;; Rule 2: A SearchOperation may lead to Detention of persons or seizure of vessels
(=>
  (and
    (instance ?S SearchOperation)
    (patient ?S ?P)
    (instance ?P Physical)
    (agent ?S ?A)
    (instance ?A AutonomousAgent)
    (property ?P Illegal))
  (permits ?A
    (exists (?D)
      (and
        (instance ?D Detention)
        (patient ?D ?P)
        (agent ?D ?A)
        (earlier
          (WhenFn ?S)
          (WhenFn ?D))))))

;; --- from item 27. HigherHeadquarters

;; Rule 3: HigherHeadquarters orders trigger permitted MilitaryOperations
(=>
  (and
    (instance ?HQ HigherHeadquarters)
    (instance ?UNIT MilitaryOrganization)
    (ancestorOrganization ?UNIT ?HQ)
    (instance ?ORD Ordering)
    (agent ?ORD ?HQ)
    (destination ?ORD ?UNIT))
  (permits ?HQ
    (exists (?OP)
      (and
        (instance ?OP MilitaryOperation)
        (agent ?OP ?UNIT)
        (causes ?ORD ?OP)))))

;; --- from item 28. OpposedBoarding

;; Rule 2: OpposedBoarding triggers UnitSelfDefense
(=>
  (and
    (instance ?OB OpposedBoarding)
    (agent ?OB ?TEAM)
    (instance ?TEAM MilitaryOrganization)
    (instance ?T HostileConduct)
    (patient ?T ?TEAM))
  (permits ?TEAM
    (exists (?SD)
      (and
        (instance ?SD UnitSelfDefense)
        (agent ?SD ?TEAM)
        (causes ?T ?SD)))))

;; Rule 3: After OpposedBoarding, Detention is permitted
(=>
  (and
    (instance ?OB OpposedBoarding)
    (agent ?OB ?TEAM)
    (instance ?TEAM MilitaryOrganization)
    (patient ?OB ?SHIP)
    (instance ?SHIP Ship))
  (permits ?TEAM
    (exists (?DET ?P)
      (and
        (instance ?DET Detention)
        (agent ?DET ?TEAM)
        (instance ?P Human)
        (patient ?DET ?P)
        (earlier
          (WhenFn ?OB)
          (WhenFn ?DET))))))

;; --- from item 32. MaritimeInterdictionOperation

;; Rule 2: MIO permits SearchOperation and Detention
(=>
  (and
    (instance ?MIO MaritimeInterdictionOperation)
    (agent ?MIO ?A)
    (instance ?A AutonomousAgent))
  (permits ?A
    (exists (?SO ?DET ?P)
      (and
        (instance ?SO SearchOperation)
        (instance ?DET Detention)
        (instance ?P Human)
        (agent ?SO ?A)
        (agent ?DET ?A)
        (patient ?DET ?P)
        (subProcess ?DET ?MIO)
        (subProcess ?SO ?MIO)
        (earlier
          (WhenFn ?SO)
          (WhenFn ?DET))))))

;; --- from item 33. VisitAndSearchOperation

;; Rule 2: If a searched person is found to be Illegal, Detention is permitted
;; as a subprocess of VisitAndSearchOperation
(=>
  (and
    (instance ?VAS VisitAndSearchOperation)
    (instance ?SO SearchOperation)
    (subProcess ?SO ?VAS)
    (agent ?SO ?A)
    (instance ?A AutonomousAgent)
    (patient ?SO ?P)
    (instance ?P Human)
    (attribute ?P Illegal))
  (permits ?A
    (exists (?DET)
      (and
        (instance ?DET Detention)
        (agent ?DET ?A)
        (patient ?DET ?P)
        (subProcess ?DET ?VAS)
        (earlier
          (WhenFn ?SO)
          (WhenFn ?DET))))))

;; --- from item 41. HorsDeCombat

;; Rule 1: HorsDeCombat person cannot be attacked
(=>
  (and
    (instance ?P Human)
    (attribute ?P HorsDeCombat)
    (instance ?A AutonomousAgent))
  (not
    (permits ?A
      (exists (?ATK)
        (and
          (instance ?ATK Attack)
          (patient ?ATK ?P)
          (agent ?ATK ?A))))))

;; --- from item 59. NonCombatant

;; Rule 1: NonCombatant cannot be targeted
(=>
  (and
    (property ?P NonCombatant)
    (instance ?A AutonomousAgent))
  (not
    (permits ?A
      (exists (?ATK)
        (and
          (instance ?ATK Attack)
          (agent ?ATK ?A)
          (patient ?ATK ?P))))))

;; --- from item 61. NoStrikeList

;; Rule 1: NoStrikeList members cannot be targeted
(=>
  (and
    (instance ?NSL NoStrikeList)
    (inList ?E ?NSL)
    (instance ?A AutonomousAgent))
  (not
    (permits ?A
      (exists (?ATK)
        (and
          (instance ?ATK Attack)
          (agent ?ATK ?A)
          (patient ?ATK ?E))))))

;; --- from item 76. WeaponsFree

;; Rule 1: Under WeaponsFree, attack on Combatant targets is permitted
(=>
  (and
    (attribute ?UNIT WeaponsFree)
    (instance ?UNIT MilitaryOrganization)
    (instance ?TARGET Physical)
    (property ?TARGET Combatant))
  (permits ?UNIT
    (exists (?ATK)
      (and
        (instance ?ATK Attack)
        (agent ?ATK ?UNIT)
        (patient ?ATK ?TARGET)))))

;; Rule 2: WeaponsFree does not override NoStrikeList protections
(=>
  (and
    (attribute ?UNIT WeaponsFree)
    (instance ?UNIT MilitaryOrganization)
    (instance ?NSL NoStrikeList)
    (inList ?TARGET ?NSL))
  (not
    (permits ?UNIT
      (exists (?ATK)
        (and
          (instance ?ATK Attack)
          (agent ?ATK ?UNIT)
          (patient ?ATK ?TARGET))))))

;; --- from item 86. FriendlyForce

;; Rule 3: FriendlyForce is not a valid target under any WeaponControlStatus
(=>
  (and
    (instance ?UNIT MilitaryOrganization)
    (instance ?WCS WeaponControlStatus)
    (attribute ?UNIT ?WCS)
    (instance ?FF FriendlyForce))
  (not
    (permits ?UNIT
      (exists (?ATK)
        (and
          (instance ?ATK Attack)
          (agent ?ATK ?UNIT)
          (patient ?ATK ?FF))))))

;; ===========================================================================
;; Legal status of flight, from ROE_Master.kif
;;
;; Four rules on entry into airspace and no-fly zones, moved here because they
;; concern the legal status of a flight rather than ROE authorization, and none
;; is used by the authorization layer. They have been typed on the way: the
;; aircraft is the instrument of the flight, not its agent, and the flight is
;; located in the airspace by &%eventLocated rather than &%meetsSpatially,
;; which relates objects; permission is stated of a formula, as &%modalAttribute
;; and &%confersNorm require. Three of them still carry a negated permission
;; condition in the antecedent. Under the open world that condition holds only
;; where a denial of permission is asserted, so the rules are inert as written;
;; if airspace enters a scenario, the condition should become an asserted
;; closure like roeNoProhibitionReaches.
;; ===========================================================================

;; --- from item 46. Airspace
;; Rule 2: Sovereign Airspace -- unauthorized entry is illegal
(=>
  (and
    (instance ?AS Airspace)
    (instance ?GA GeographicArea)
    (orientation ?AS ?GA Above)
    (instance ?STATE Nation)
    (claimedTerritory ?GA ?STATE)
    (instance ?AC Aircraft)
    (instance ?T Flying)
    (instrument ?T ?AC)
    (eventLocated ?T ?AS)
    (not
      (confersNorm ?STATE Permission
        (eventLocated ?T ?AS))))
  (property ?T Illegal))

;; --- from item 47. InternationalAirspace
;; Rule 3: Transit freedom
(=>
  (and
    (instance ?IA InternationalAirspace)
    (instance ?AC Aircraft)
    (instance ?T Translocation)
    (instrument ?T ?AC)
    (eventLocated ?T ?IA))
  (modalAttribute
    (eventLocated ?T ?IA) Permission))

;; --- from item 54. NationalAirspace
;; Rule 2: NationalAirspace is sovereign -- unauthorized entry is Illegal
(=>
  (and
    (instance ?NA NationalAirspace)
    (instance ?AC Aircraft)
    (instance ?T Flying)
    (instrument ?T ?AC)
    (eventLocated ?T ?NA)
    (not
      (modalAttribute
        (eventLocated ?T ?NA) Permission)))
  (property ?T Illegal))

;; --- from item 57. NoFlyZone
;; Rule 2: Unauthorized flying in a NoFlyZone is Illegal
(=>
  (and
    (instance ?NFZ NoFlyZone)
    (instance ?F Flying)
    (eventLocated ?F ?NFZ)
    (not
      (modalAttribute
        (eventLocated ?F ?NFZ) Permission)))
  (property ?F Illegal))
